From a0c54675aee7eaed3b98ed78db8c0587076a0683 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: stevostar1234 <skybasteven@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 22 Sep 2026 20:37:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Second-opinion follow-up for the public form hardening

- Website check: after "https://" is added, the browser's own URL check
  accepted almost anything ("https://N/A", "https://someone@example.org",
  "https://not a website" in Chromium 152), so values the old engine blocked
  were posted as the organisation's website and echoed in emails. A website
  now needs an http(s) address with a real host name and no user name; a
  rejected entry is left as typed. Bare host names such as www.example.org,
  ports and international domains are still accepted.
- The submit control is enabled at the end of each form's set-up, and only
  when its system fields were filled, rather than before the Code of Conduct,
  honeypot and submit handler were installed.
- Each NTE form gains a short noscript notice with the existing NTE contact
  address (the 16 September O03 fix asked for a no-JavaScript route).
- The volunteer form belongs to the Mission app: its submit control and the
  volunteer thank-you page are left as they were.
- Tests: every form is checked for re-enabling, junk website values and the
  noscript notice are covered.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
